Final mechanism:
- transport.rs socket_ownership holds an exclusive nonblocking flock on persistent <socket>.lock. File is retained in BoundSocket, so descriptor close (including inherited descriptors) governs ownership. The lock file is never unlinked or truncated; every later owner locks the same inode.
- Lock must be an owned regular file of exact0600, checked before/after open. O_NOFOLLOW, O_CLOEXEC and O_NONBLOCK apply; device/inode is checked across open and lock. Interrupted flock retries; contention returns AddrInUse with the real OS error and named live-supervisor reason.
- Existing endpoint without lock returns ControlSocketOwnershipUnknown, before creating any lock. Repeated refusals cannot mint reclaim authority. This intentional fail-closed legacy behavior is expressly approved for dev/no installed base.
- Only exclusive lock ownership authorizes stale owned-socket unlink/rebind. Existing file-type, uid and inode guards remain. Connect errno is no longer liveness authority: a saturated live listener can return ECONNREFUSED here.
- Tests cover ordinary live endpoint/file protection, inherited CLOEXEC listener+lock, full backlog, persistent inode/content/private permissions, legacy live and orphan refusal, and wrong mode/type/symlink/expected uid. Wrong-uid fixture passes a different expected uid to the private helper; no chown or privileged state.

